> 🤖 This issue was generated by Claude on behalf of @keddie.

#2536 (PR #2544) makes `FakeSeam`'s **constructor** refuse a roster without `selfId`. Two doors reach the identical state afterwards, so the guard is a **construction-time snapshot rather than an invariant**.

## Door 1 — `removePeer(selfId)` (the real one)

`FakeSeam.kt:157`:

```kotlin
/** Remove [peer] from the live peers set. */
public fun removePeer(peer: PeerId) {
    _peers.update { it - peer }
}
```

`FakeSeam(selfId = a, initialPeers = setOf(a, b)).removePeer(a)` yields `_peers == {b}` with `state` still `Woven` — **bit-identical** to what the new `require` refuses, reached from a consumer's test *body* instead of its fixture. The inversion #2536 exists to prevent fires exactly as described: one live remote, `peers.value.size > 1` is `false`, and a consumer keying on the contract's documented sentinel reads "alone".

Concrete scenario: a consumer simulating a full membership drain writes `seam.peers.value.forEach { seam.removePeer(it) }` — the natural spelling, and the shape `MidHandshakeCollapse` already models one peer at a time. They get `emptySet()`, and every downstream assertion on `selfId in peers` or `size > 1` silently describes something production cannot produce.

**Fix — three lines, zero in-tree cost.** All 19 `removePeer` call sites and both `assertAbortsOnMidHandshakeCollapse` call sites were checked during #2544's review; **none passes `selfId`**.

```kotlin
public fun removePeer(peer: PeerId) {
    require(peer != selfId) { "A Seam's peers always contains selfId … use tear() to collapse to { selfId }" }
    _peers.update { it - peer }
}
```

